  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Direct measured oral bioavailability in humans/rats for the closest related polyphenolic natural products is consistently below the 20% threshold: chrysin <1% (C02) and predicted 0.003–0.02% (C16); peonidin and petunidin glycosides 0.14–0.48% (C01, C03, C04, C05, C10, C11, C12, C17); curcumin poor/low (C07, C13, C14, C19); flavonolignans very low by CL/F (C06, C08, C09, C15, C18).",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C16","C01","C03","C04","C05","C10","C11","C12","C17","C07","C13","C14","C19","C06","C08","C09","C15","C18"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 context reinforces the low-F pattern: acacetin has poor oral bioavailability due to glucuronidation and BCRP/MRP2 efflux (C25), curcumin is very low with only traces systemically available (C24, C29), and even the relatively better-absorbed 7,8-benzoflavone is 13.2% in rats (C26), below 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C25","C24","C29","C26"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Scopoletin is a small monophenolic coumarin with favorable physicochemical priors, but it retains a phenolic OH prone to first-pass conjugation and lacks any direct F>=20% evidence; the transferable low-F evidence from related phenolic natural products is the only endpoint-relevant empirical basis.",
      "card_ids": ["C02","C16","C25","C26"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
